@* Gates content behind a desktop-width viewport.

   The admin screens are wide data tables and a month calendar — they are unusable on a phone,
   and a mis-tap there can add a strike or delete a member. This is a usability gate, not a
   security boundary: access control is the [Authorize] attribute on the page.

   Deliberately a CSS breakpoint rather than User-Agent sniffing, so it also catches a
   half-width laptop window, where the table is just as unusable. No JS, so pages using it
   can still be static SSR. *@

<div class="lg:hidden">
    <div class="spe-page-shell md:max-w-180 text-center">
        <div class="mx-auto mb-5 flex h-14 w-14 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-spe-cobalt/10 text-spe-cobalt">
            <Icon Name="desktop" Class="h-7 w-7" />
        </div>
        <p class="spe-page-kicker">Desktop only</p>
        <h1 class="spe-page-title">@Title</h1>
        <p class="mx-auto mt-3 max-w-md text-sm text-slate-600">
            This screen needs a wider window than your device has. Open it on a laptop or desktop —
            or widen this window if you're already on one.
        </p>
        <a href="/" class="spe-primary-btn mt-6 inline-block">Back to Home</a>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="hidden lg:block">
    @ChildContent
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Name of the gated screen, shown in the narrow-viewport notice.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string Title { get; set; } = "Admin";
}
